sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/hollow-tube-mass-optimality--gemini-4-argon--1> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "Pre-registered test of R/t ratios against Brazier buckling across 120 specimens" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/hollow-tube-mass-optimality> ;
    dct:description "Assemble a pre-registered dataset of 120 specimens spanning four biological hollow structure types: vertebrate long bones (avian wing humeri/ulnae and terrestrial mammalian femora from Dryad and MorphoSource), grass and reed stems, bamboo culms, and avian feather rachises, paired with Currey and Alexander's (1985) 220-bone tabular compilation. For each specimen, extract mid-shaft outer radius R, wall thickness t, effective internal core density rho_core / rho_solid (accounting for marrow, trabecular foam, or parenchymatous pith), and longitudinal tissue modulus and yield strain (sigma_y / E).\n\nTest three competing mechanical predictions for the dimensionless ratio k = R/t: (1) pure empty-tube stiffness-per-mass maximization, which is unbounded unless regularized by core mass (predicting k_opt = [(3/2)(rho_solid / rho_core - 1)]^(1/2)); (2) the Brazier ovalization and local shell-buckling limit under bending, which caps k at k_buckling = C_B * (E / sigma_y) with C_B approx 0.3; and (3) constant minimum wall thickness for impact resistance. Compare residuals across the four structure types and test allometric scaling of k with body or organ size." ;
    schema:additionalProperty <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/hollow-tube-mass-optimality--gemini-4-argon--1#n_specimens> ;
    delphi:proposedTest "Across the 120 specimens, a Bayes factor > 10 (or AIC difference > 10) decisively separating the core-mass-regularized stiffness-per-mass optimum k_opt from the Brazier local-buckling ceiling k_buckling within each of the four structure classes." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: Observed R/t ratios across the four biological classes show high variance uncorrelated with either rho_solid / rho_core or E / sigma_y (R^2 < 0.15), refuting both mechanical optimality models." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/data-test" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Compile and checksum 120 specimens across long bones (including Dryad 10.5061/dryad.7vf4004 and 10.5061/dryad.9ct2f), plant stems, bamboo culms, and feather rachises with matched tissue E, sigma_y, and core density. 2. Pre-register the predicted loci k_opt(rho_core / rho_solid) and k_buckling(E / sigma_y) and a tolerance band of +/- 25% in log(R/t). 3. Compute the log-ratio deviation from both boundaries for each structure class and fit a phylogenetic/taxonomic mixed model testing size dependence. 4. Compare marrow-filled terrestrial bones against pneumatized avian wing bones and foam-filled plant stems to test whether core density quantitatively explains the shift in R/t." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): tractable" ;
    skos:note "Tool: morphometry" ;
    skos:note "Tool: mechanics-modelling" ;
    skos:note "Tool: data-test" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch16-03> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch16-04> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://datadryad.org/dataset/doi:10.5061/dryad.7vf4004> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://datadryad.org/dataset/doi:10.5061/dryad.9ct2f> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://www.morphosource.org/> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1469-7998.1985.tb03551.x> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1098/rspa.1995.0076> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1098/rspa.1995.0075> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1098/rspa.1927.0125> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/gemini-4-argon.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/hollow-tube-mass-optimality> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "Are biological hollow structures at the stiffness-per-mass optimum?" .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/hollow-tube-mass-optimality--gemini-4-argon--1#n_specimens> a schema:PropertyValue ;
    schema:propertyID "n_specimens" ;
    schema:value 120 ;
    schema:description "specimens named in the text (a sample size as stated there, not a measurement made here)" .
  <https://datadryad.org/dataset/doi:10.5061/dryad.7vf4004> dct:title "Dryad long-bone cross-sectional morphology dataset (10.5061/dryad.7vf4004)" ;
    dct:description "Cross-sectional geometric properties and cortical dimensions of mammalian long bones" .
  <https://datadryad.org/dataset/doi:10.5061/dryad.9ct2f> dct:title "Dryad vertebrate long-bone FE and beam theory dataset (10.5061/dryad.9ct2f)" ;
    dct:description "Morphologically diverse sample of vertebrate long bones with CT cross-sectional geometry" .
  <https://www.morphosource.org/> dct:title "MorphoSource 3D vertebrate skeletal CT archive" ;
    dct:description "Open micro-CT scans of avian pneumatized wing bones and terrestrial femora" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1469-7998.1985.tb03551.x> dct:title "The thickness of the walls of tubular bones" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Currey and Alexander 1985, The thickness of the walls of tubular bones" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1098/rspa.1995.0076> dct:title "The mechanical properties of natural materials. II. Microstructures for mechanical efficiency" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Gibson, Ashby, Wegst and Olive 1995, The mechanical properties of natural materials. II. Microstructures for mechanical efficiency" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1098/rspa.1995.0075> dct:title "The mechanical properties of natural materials. I. Material property charts" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Ashby, Gibson, Wegst and Olive 1995, The mechanical properties of natural materials. I. Material property charts" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1098/rspa.1927.0125> dct:title "On the flexure of thin cylindrical shells and other \"thin\" sections" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Brazier 1927, On the flexure of thin cylindrical shells and other thin sections" .
}

